## 6. Backend

The backend is a Node.js/Express application. Its entry point is `server.js` and its package script is:

```json
"start": "node server.js"
```

The server first initializes Redis support, connects to MongoDB, and only then starts the Express listener. Therefore, a MongoDB connectivity failure prevents the HTTP API from starting.

The API routes are mounted under:

```text
/api/posts
/api/auth
/api/user
```

The application also has a root endpoint:

```text
GET /
```

that returns a basic backend-accessibility message.

### Backend Dockerfile

The repository's backend Dockerfile uses a builder stage followed by a runtime stage:

```dockerfile
FROM node:22-slim AS builder
WORKDIR /app
COPY . .
RUN npm i

FROM node:22-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=builder /app .
COPY .env .env
EXPOSE 5000
CMD ["npm","start"]
```

### Backend container

Example deployment command:

```bash
docker run -d \
  --name mern-backend \
  --network mern-network \
  -p 5000:5000 \
  -e PORT=5000 \
  -e MONGODB_URI="mongodb://mongo:27017/wanderlust" \
  mern-backend:latest
```

### Backend endpoint

```text
http://<EC2_PUBLIC_IP>:5000/api/posts
```

A successful response may show `200 OK`; a `304 Not Modified` response is also a valid HTTP response indicating the client can use its cached representation when the resource has not changed.

